T:What it is
T:A river is a
T:natural
T:stream of
T:fresh water
T:that flows
T:on land or
T:inside caves
T:towards
T:another body
T:of water at
T:a lower
T:elevation,
T:such as an
T:ocean, lake,
T:or another
T:river. A
T:river may
T:run dry
T:before
T:reaching the
T:end of its
T:course if it
T:runs out of
T:water, or
T:only flow
T:during
T:certain
T:seasons.
T:Rivers are
T:regulated by
T:the water
T:cycle, the
T:processes by
T:which water
T:moves around
T:the Earth.
T:Water first
T:enters
T:rivers
T:through
T:precipitati,
T:whether from
T:rainfall,
T:the runoff
T:of water
T:down a
T:slope, the
T:melting of
T:glaciers or
T:snow, or
T:seepage from
T:aquifers
T:beneath the
T:surface of
T:the Earth.
